WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.808 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:05.808 --> 00:00:12.590 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.640 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yes, of
course, if we argue--

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.190 align:middle line:84%
if we want to prove to him
that x is in the language,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.340 align:middle line:84%
it's essentially
we're telling him,

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.770 align:middle line:84%
if you evaluate x on
circuit C, you will get 1.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.160 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe if there
exists a witness

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.260 align:middle line:84%
that C of the witness C and x
and the witness gives you 1.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.590 align:middle line:84%
Of course, we assume the
verifier knows the description

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:31.970 align:middle line:90%
of this circuit C.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.730 align:middle line:84%
And usually, and
for our purposes,

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.760 align:middle line:84%
you think of C as being a
uniform, like a Turing machine.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.750 align:middle line:84%
So it can be
generated efficiently

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:42.960 align:middle line:90%
from a Turing machine.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.320 align:middle line:84%
But yes, the verifier
can generate--

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.520 align:middle line:84%
both the prover and verifier
can generate the circuit.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.342 align:middle line:84%
So if you think of
three SAT, the circuit

00:00:53.342 --> 00:00:54.800 align:middle line:84%
takes as input all
the assignments,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.488 align:middle line:84%
and it checks that all
the clauses are satisfied.

00:01:00.488 --> 00:01:01.780 align:middle line:90%
That's an example of a circuit.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:02.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah,

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But also
checking [INAUDIBLE],

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.265 align:middle line:84%
isn't that quite similar to
just running that circuit?

00:01:09.265 --> 00:01:10.890 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, it's
like just-- yeah.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.480 align:middle line:84%
So the prover
essentially computes

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.580 align:middle line:84%
the circuit, all the
gates in the circuit,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.300 align:middle line:84%
hashes it, and then
gives a BARG that each

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.480 align:middle line:84%
and every gate of the
circuit is satisfied,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.550 align:middle line:84%
that the value he
hashed satisfy each--

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.427 align:middle line:90%
respect each gate.

00:01:29.427 --> 00:01:31.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It seems like
because the verifier

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.790 align:middle line:84%
is going to also check
if each of those bits,

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.400 align:middle line:90%
corresponding in B0 B1.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.240 align:middle line:90%
Because want to be out.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.410 align:middle line:84%
Seems like that among
those running the gate.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.510 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So the verifier
doesn't know all these values,

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.660 align:middle line:90%
all these bits.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.040 align:middle line:84%
The verifier only
knows the hash.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.830 align:middle line:84%
He gets only v. He doesn't know
all these things, and he can't.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:55.955 align:middle line:90%
There are too many of them.

00:01:55.955 --> 00:01:58.500 align:middle line:90%


00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.000 align:middle line:84%
He knows what the
circuit is, but he

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000 align:middle line:84%
doesn't run in time that
depends on the circuit.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.010 align:middle line:90%
The circuit is too big.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.640 align:middle line:84%
He just knows in his
head how to generate

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.800 align:middle line:90%
each gate in the circuit.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.759 align:middle line:90%
So he doesn't know these bits.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:14.555 align:middle line:84%
All he knows is the hash value
and the BARG, which is succinct.

00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:18.900 align:middle line:90%


00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.983 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Security
is going to give us

00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:22.210 align:middle line:90%
some lower bound on L, right?

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.445 align:middle line:90%
It just happens?

00:02:23.445 --> 00:02:24.820 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: A lower
bound on what?

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:25.380 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: On L.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.220 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: On L, yes.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:26.700 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, OK.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.890 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, we're
going to-- yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.932 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to talk
about security soundness,

00:02:30.932 --> 00:02:31.800 align:middle line:90%
so we'll see.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.100 align:middle line:84%
One thing I want to say before
we jump to soundness-- just

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:40.260 align:middle line:84%
note, because I said
the CRS of the BARG,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.110 align:middle line:84%
we generated with security
parameter lambda, k,

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.360 align:middle line:84%
which is the number of gates,
and then I said n prime.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:46.860 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.300 align:middle line:90%
We'll see what n prime is.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.590 align:middle line:90%
I forgot to-- this is n prime.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:54.270 align:middle line:90%
It's just security parameter.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.320 align:middle line:84%
N prime will be the
length of the hash key,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.870 align:middle line:84%
the length of v,
which grows with L,

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.450 align:middle line:90%
and J. That's the n prime.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:07.000 align:middle line:84%
I'm just mentioning it because
I forgot to circle back to it.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.460 align:middle line:84%
OK, are you ready to talk
about analyzing this?

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.961 align:middle line:84%
Because that's actually
the very interesting part.

00:03:13.961 --> 00:03:15.340 align:middle line:90%
So let's analyze.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:20.140 align:middle line:90%


00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.295 align:middle line:84%
OK, we need to argue
completeness and soundness.

00:03:24.295 --> 00:03:26.170 align:middle line:84%
Completeness-- I don't
want to waste our time

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.340 align:middle line:90%
because it's just trivial.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.800 align:middle line:84%
Completeness just follows from
the completeness of the BARG

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:35.120 align:middle line:84%
and completeness of
some extractable hash.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.660 align:middle line:84%
So essentially, if you're
honest, if x is in the language,

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.610 align:middle line:84%
you compute all the
wires correctly,

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.770 align:middle line:90%
you hashed all the wires.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.810 align:middle line:84%
And if you hashed all
the wires correctly,

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.990 align:middle line:84%
then you have a valid
witness for all the wires.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:52.460 align:middle line:90%
So your BARG will be accepted.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.880 align:middle line:84%
If you did everything
correctly, everything

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.220 align:middle line:84%
is going to be accepted
because the completeness

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.870 align:middle line:90%
of the underlying primitive.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.500 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing
interesting going on.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.380 align:middle line:84%
The interesting
part is soundness.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:06.610 align:middle line:84%
So how do we know
you cannot cheat?

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:12.970 align:middle line:84%
And let me even
jump and tell you,

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.670 align:middle line:90%
this thing is not really sound.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:16.640 align:middle line:90%
But--

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.010 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:19.510 align:middle line:90%


00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.030 align:middle line:90%
But we'll make it sound.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.280 align:middle line:90%
So what do we know?

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.670 align:middle line:84%
So soundness-- so what
do we want to prove?

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:33.880 align:middle line:90%


00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.660 align:middle line:84%
Let's try to hope
for-- so again,

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.370 align:middle line:84%
there's nonadaptive
and adaptive.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:38.480 align:middle line:90%
You can think of both.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.855 align:middle line:84%
But let's talk about, what
does it mean to be, let's say,

00:04:40.855 --> 00:04:41.750 align:middle line:90%
adaptively sound?

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:49.960 align:middle line:84%
So we need to prove that for any
polynomial-size cheating prover,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:56.780 align:middle line:84%
we want to say the probability
that P star takes a CRS

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.650 align:middle line:84%
of the BARG-- of the SNARG--
so a hash key and a CRS

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:01.610 align:middle line:90%
for the BARG--

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:08.750 align:middle line:84%
it takes the CRS, and it
outputs some x and pi such

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:11.690 align:middle line:90%
that x is not in the language.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:23.810 align:middle line:84%
And pi, the SNARG is satisfied
v and CRS x pi outputs 1.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.556 align:middle line:84%
You want to say that
this is negligible.

00:05:26.556 --> 00:05:29.300 align:middle line:90%


00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.040 align:middle line:90%
That's what we need to prove.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.690 align:middle line:84%
The probability that a
cheating prover gets a CRS

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.430 align:middle line:84%
and generates any x
that's not in the language

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:43.220 align:middle line:90%
and a proof that's accepted--

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:44.490 align:middle line:90%
you can't do that.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.360 align:middle line:84%
You can do that only with
the Sanger probability.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.450 align:middle line:84%
Let me just mention another
more weaker definition is

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:57.000 align:middle line:84%
the nonadaptive, which says,
for any P star and for every x--

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.820 align:middle line:90%
so he gives x ahead of time.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.870 align:middle line:84%
For any P star and for
any x not in the language,

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:05.270 align:middle line:84%
when he gets a random
CRS, the probability

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:09.410 align:middle line:84%
that he managed to generate
a fake proof that's accepted

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:10.543 align:middle line:90%
is negligible.

00:06:10.543 --> 00:06:11.960 align:middle line:84%
So there's two
variants-- one when

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:16.830 align:middle line:84%
he needs to choose a
given x for all the CRSes,

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:20.030 align:middle line:84%
or he can choose an x
depending on the CRS.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.820 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk about the adaptive
because it's just stronger,

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.240 align:middle line:84%
and we can get it, at
least in some cases.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.050 align:middle line:84%
So today, the SNARGs is
in the literature-- so

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.280 align:middle line:84%
achieve adaptive, some
achieve nonadaptive.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.610 align:middle line:84%
And in general, the ones for
deterministic computations

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.000 align:middle line:90%
are adaptive.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.850 align:middle line:84%
The one for nondeterministic
computations

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.600 align:middle line:84%
are more on the
nonadaptive side.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.570 align:middle line:84%
And we don't have
SNARGs for all of NP.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.030 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of the talk,
at the end of the class,

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.510 align:middle line:84%
I'll leave 10 minutes
to talk about where

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.410 align:middle line:90%
we're standing as a community.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:57.300 align:middle line:84%
But both definitions
are relevant,

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.670 align:middle line:84%
and we have results in both
settings-- the adaptive

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:00.790 align:middle line:90%
and the nonadaptive.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:05.770 align:middle line:84%
But let's talk today on
the adaptive setting.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.570 align:middle line:90%
So that's what we want.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:10.450 align:middle line:84%
But the question,
do we get this?

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:16.000 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to see what this
construction actually gives us.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.100 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to argue
is actually this construction

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.150 align:middle line:90%
does not give a soundness.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:30.460 align:middle line:84%
What it gives us is a
form of local soundness.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.990 align:middle line:90%
So I'll explain what this means.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:39.630 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to argue
is that-- so claim the SNARG

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:43.710 align:middle line:84%
that I just constructed
is, let me call it,

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:49.990 align:middle line:84%
locally sound or
locally consistent.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:54.090 align:middle line:84%
It's also people call
locally consistent.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:55.610 align:middle line:90%
And I'll explain what it is.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:58.430 align:middle line:90%


00:07:58.430 --> 00:07:59.830 align:middle line:90%
We want to prove soundness.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:01.270 align:middle line:90%
So what do we want to prove?

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:06.570 align:middle line:84%
We want to prove that
this SNARG, if you manage

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:08.520 align:middle line:84%
to say that the
output is 1, there's

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.370 align:middle line:84%
actually an assignment to all
the wires that give you 1.

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:14.040 align:middle line:90%
That means it's sound.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:16.290 align:middle line:90%
We can't prove that.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.110 align:middle line:90%
What can we prove?

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:24.390 align:middle line:84%
We can prove that if you
argue that the value is 1,

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:28.080 align:middle line:84%
I can use you to
extract the value

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.150 align:middle line:90%
of any small set of wires--

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.590 align:middle line:84%
how many L. So
remember, our SNARG,

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:37.480 align:middle line:90%
we're binding on allocations.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.610 align:middle line:84%
These allocations,
I can extract.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:42.659 align:middle line:84%
You gave me a V that's
binding allocation.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.280 align:middle line:90%
I can extract.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.340 align:middle line:84%
I can get from you
the L wire values.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.990 align:middle line:84%
So I get L wires
values from you.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.620 align:middle line:84%
What I can argue
about these L wire

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:57.530 align:middle line:84%
values is that locally
they're consistent.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:01.630 align:middle line:84%
So if there's a gate,
if the L wire values--

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:04.040 align:middle line:84%
let's say three of them
are connected with a gate--

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.300 align:middle line:84%
I can argue that this
gate is satisfied.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:12.940 align:middle line:84%
So I can argue this form
of local consistency.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.380 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to define
what local consistency is.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:19.150 align:middle line:84%
But just high level,
what I can argue

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:23.650 align:middle line:84%
is not that I have a
assignment of all the wires

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:25.900 align:middle line:84%
that are consistent
inside, and the output

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.640 align:middle line:84%
is 1, which is what
I want to argue.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.670 align:middle line:84%
I can argue that I
have an assignment

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:36.230 align:middle line:84%
that I can have a small window
to the wires, a window of size

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:36.730 align:middle line:90%
L.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:40.593 align:middle line:84%
So I can look at these L wires,
and they look consistent.

00:09:40.593 --> 00:09:43.010 align:middle line:84%
And I can look at these L
wires, and they look consistent.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:45.520 align:middle line:90%
And the output is 1.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.272 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like, OK,
local now, we only

00:09:48.272 --> 00:09:49.730 align:middle line:84%
need to stitch all
of them together

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:52.310 align:middle line:84%
and to get a final
assignment, and we're done.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:55.610 align:middle line:84%
So the goal is we're going to
say, locally we're consistent.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:59.100 align:middle line:84%
And local consistency
implies global consistency.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.400 align:middle line:90%
And so we get soundness.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.000 align:middle line:84%
So the plan-- we are going
to prove local consistency.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.050 align:middle line:90%
So that's check.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.190 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
define it and prove it.

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:13.100 align:middle line:84%
The local consistency
implies global.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:17.070 align:middle line:84%
That turns out to be much more
difficult than we thought.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.820 align:middle line:84%
So when we started working
on this, we were like, oh,

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:20.730 align:middle line:90%
local consistency.

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.100 align:middle line:90%
But we shouldn't apply global.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.070 align:middle line:90%
That should be good.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:24.960 align:middle line:84%
So we worked really
hard local, local.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.420 align:middle line:84%
And then turns out,
local to global

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.140 align:middle line:90%
was another 10 years of work.

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:30.400 align:middle line:84%
And we're in
progress, in progress.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.150 align:middle line:90%


00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.600 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going to see today
is how to get local consistency.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:40.070 align:middle line:84%
We're going to talk a little bit
about why local implies global

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:42.420 align:middle line:90%
and why not.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.610 align:middle line:84%
Why doesn't it
always imply global?

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.260 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, it will give
you some food for thought.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.480 align:middle line:90%


00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.455 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to argue
that it's locally sound.

00:10:52.455 --> 00:10:54.330 align:middle line:84%
And let me tell you what
locally sound means.

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.320 align:middle line:90%
Let me first define it.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:06.860 align:middle line:84%
So the definition-- so
here's what it means.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.600 align:middle line:90%
It means that there exists--

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.550 align:middle line:84%
so I'm now defining
what this means.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.510 align:middle line:84%
It means that there
exists an extractor--

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:18.890 align:middle line:84%
what we call sometimes
a local assignment--

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:23.244 align:middle line:84%
so some extractor,
a local assignment--

00:11:23.244 --> 00:11:26.450 align:middle line:90%


00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:30.770 align:middle line:84%
generator, that's
another name we use--

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:36.810 align:middle line:84%
E-- so we have an extractor that
generates assignments for us.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.250 align:middle line:84%
This assignment takes only L
wire values on the circuit.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:48.930 align:middle line:84%
So we have a circuit C. This
extractor or local assignment

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.360 align:middle line:90%
generator, it gets L wires--

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.570 align:middle line:90%
i1 up to il.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:59.790 align:middle line:84%
They're all in the wires,
which we denoted by n.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:04.500 align:middle line:84%
So it takes l wires,
and it outputs

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.630 align:middle line:84%
some x star in an
instance and wire values--

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:14.080 align:middle line:90%
and values wi1 up to wil.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.850 align:middle line:84%
This is what our local
assignment generator outputs.

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:23.640 align:middle line:84%
For any i1 up to il, it outputs
some instance and y values

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:38.100 align:middle line:84%
that satisfy the following such
that 1 is local consistency,

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:40.980 align:middle line:90%
which means any two wires--

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:46.080 align:middle line:84%
any wires that are connected
by a gate satisfy the gate.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.700 align:middle line:90%
And the output wire is a 1.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:52.030 align:middle line:84%
And if one of them are
connected to input wires,

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.930 align:middle line:84%
then it's consistent
with the input wire.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.060 align:middle line:84%
So local consistency
just means it

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:08.430 align:middle line:84%
respects all gates
consistent with x1 up

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:14.510 align:middle line:90%
to xn and out and output one.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.960 align:middle line:90%


00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:22.260 align:middle line:84%
So of course, if you just
read wires here and here,

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.560 align:middle line:84%
like very spread, they are
not connected with any gates,

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:29.280 align:middle line:84%
then the local consistency
doesn't say anything

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.380 align:middle line:84%
because you can't determine
the value of any gate.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.780 align:middle line:84%
But any gate for
which you have--

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.300 align:middle line:84%
any gate for which
the input and output

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:39.030 align:middle line:84%
wires are these
or the output wire

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:44.500 align:middle line:84%
or the input wires, the
gate should be satisfied.

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:45.950 align:middle line:90%
That's number 1.

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:50.941 align:middle line:84%
And number 2 is what
we call nonsignaling.

00:13:50.941 --> 00:13:51.483 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain.

00:13:51.483 --> 00:13:57.610 align:middle line:90%


00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:01.060 align:middle line:84%
The number 2 says
the wire values

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:05.320 align:middle line:84%
that he gives here, that the
extractor, the local assignment

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:09.370 align:middle line:84%
generator gives, the
distribution-- so this

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:10.970 align:middle line:90%
is a randomized algorithm.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:15.310 align:middle line:84%
This extractor is a
randomized algorithm.

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:18.100 align:middle line:84%
Given these wire, he
gives a distribution

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:21.580 align:middle line:90%
over x star and wire values.

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:25.640 align:middle line:84%
The claim is the distribution
of x star, for example,

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:28.300 align:middle line:90%
doesn't depend on i1 up to il.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:32.890 align:middle line:84%
So the distribution of x
star in this computationally

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:37.700 align:middle line:84%
looks the same, no matter
which wires you give.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:41.950 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, the
distribution of wi1

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:44.650 align:middle line:90%
doesn't depend on i2 up to il.

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:47.350 align:middle line:90%
So let me say this formally.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:58.030 align:middle line:84%
Formally, for every
set I and J of size l--

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:58.930 align:middle line:90%
let me-- sorry.

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:07.460 align:middle line:84%
For any I and J subset of n-- so
for any set of wires of size l--

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:11.295 align:middle line:84%
so take any set of
wires, like i1 to il.

00:15:11.295 --> 00:15:15.940 align:middle line:84%
Let me call the first
set I, the second set J.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:21.140 align:middle line:84%
And what we know is if you
look at the intersection of--

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:22.365 align:middle line:90%
so if you look at--

00:15:22.365 --> 00:15:27.190 align:middle line:90%


00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:28.870 align:middle line:90%
how will I-- one second.

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:31.435 align:middle line:90%
Let me just think of notation.

00:15:31.435 --> 00:15:37.390 align:middle line:84%
If you look at extractor
an I but only restricted

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:42.710 align:middle line:84%
to the coordinate in I
intersect J. So the w's--

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:43.440 align:middle line:90%
there's many.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.900 align:middle line:90%
There's w for i1, i2.

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:48.350 align:middle line:84%
Let's look only at the
w's in the locations

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:52.670 align:middle line:90%
that are both in I and in J.

00:15:52.670 --> 00:16:02.150 align:middle line:84%
The distribution of them looks
like-- it looks at in distinct

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:03.450 align:middle line:90%
from the distribution.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:08.420 align:middle line:84%
If you looked at these
coordinates with set

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:13.740 align:middle line:84%
J. In other words, let's say
the intersection of I and J are,

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.690 align:middle line:90%
I don't know, some set of wires.

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.540 align:middle line:84%
Put the set of wires
in the beginning.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:21.110 align:middle line:90%


00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.070 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at
a set of wires,

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.860 align:middle line:90%
look at their distribution.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:28.700 align:middle line:84%
Their distribution
does not depend

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:31.670 align:middle line:90%
on what the other wires are.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:33.960 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the
distribution of these,

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:37.490 align:middle line:84%
they will look the same whether
the rest of the i's were

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:39.800 align:middle line:90%
of one form or another form.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:41.760 align:middle line:84%
At least computationally,
they look the same.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:44.160 align:middle line:84%
They need not be
exactly the same.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:47.360 align:middle line:84%
But nobody can distinguish
between the two.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:52.500 align:middle line:84%
And this is
including the x star.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:58.100 align:middle line:84%
So E is like when the
extractor here, it

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:09.410 align:middle line:84%
means x star and the
wij for I, J in i.

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:11.040 align:middle line:90%
So I include the x star.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.560 align:middle line:84%
So even with x star,
the x star here

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:15.950 align:middle line:84%
and the x star here
are indistinguishable.

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:17.359 align:middle line:90%
X star, I always include.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:21.480 align:middle line:84%
Even if it's empty,
I include x star.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:22.710 align:middle line:90%
So that's a nonsignaling.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:25.339 align:middle line:84%
The distributions are
indistinguishable.

00:17:25.339 --> 00:17:29.600 align:middle line:84%
And now I want to
argue that we get--

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:30.663 align:middle line:90%
I have this extractor.

00:17:30.663 --> 00:17:31.580 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to show you.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:32.525 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to construct
this extractor.

00:17:32.525 --> 00:17:35.070 align:middle line:84%
It's actually very easy to
construct this extractor.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:38.160 align:middle line:84%
So if you give me
a cheating prover,

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:39.380 align:middle line:90%
if you give me any prover.

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:40.297 align:middle line:90%
Forgot about cheating.

00:17:40.297 --> 00:17:42.990 align:middle line:84%
You give me a prover, I can
construct this local assignment

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:44.880 align:middle line:90%
generator from him.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And then all I need to do is
argue that a local assignment

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:53.410 align:middle line:84%
generate implies that I
have an assignment for all,

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.177 align:middle line:84%
like, not just
locally consistent,

00:17:56.177 --> 00:17:58.260 align:middle line:84%
but a fully consistent
assignment to all the wires

00:17:58.260 --> 00:17:59.710 align:middle line:90%
that give output 1.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:02.250 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, x needs
to be in the language.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:03.553 align:middle line:90%
That's the proof strategy.

00:18:03.553 --> 00:18:06.240 align:middle line:90%


00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:10.390 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to do first
is show you how we get--

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:13.350 align:middle line:84%
so we're going to show
how, given any p star,

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:17.070 align:middle line:84%
we can generate a local
assignment generate.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:19.740 align:middle line:84%
We can give local
assignment to all the wires

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:23.100 align:middle line:84%
that are consistent
and satisfy this kind

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.300 align:middle line:90%
of nonsignaling property.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:27.930 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to show how
to use this local assignment

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:31.650 align:middle line:90%
generator to get soundness.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:32.490 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:33.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:39.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is another issue with
obtaining this soundness comes

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:44.477 align:middle line:90%
from the left side of BARG?

00:18:44.477 --> 00:18:46.810 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Actually, the
semi-adaptive soundness of BARG

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:47.930 align:middle line:90%
is enough for me.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:48.710 align:middle line:90%
It's enough.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:50.390 align:middle line:90%
I don't need to use--

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:52.160 align:middle line:90%
you'll see, actually.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:55.245 align:middle line:84%
I don't need full
adaptive BARGs.

00:18:55.245 --> 00:18:56.830 align:middle line:90%
I never use it.

00:18:56.830 --> 00:19:00.320 align:middle line:84%
It's enough for me that the
BARG only has semi even though--

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:01.520 align:middle line:90%
OK, that's a good point.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:03.870 align:middle line:84%
Note, I'm going to prove
adaptive soundness.

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:05.620 align:middle line:84%
And you're saying,
wait, but the BARG only

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.330 align:middle line:90%
has semi-adaptive soundness.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:08.330 align:middle line:90%
Is that enough?

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:10.180 align:middle line:84%
Is it enough to use
only semi-adaptive BARG

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.170 align:middle line:90%
and get fully adaptive SNARG?

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:13.270 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:16.570 align:middle line:84%
I will not need the full
adaptivity of the BARG.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:17.950 align:middle line:90%
We'll see it next.

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:18.530 align:middle line:90%
I'll use it.

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:20.060 align:middle line:90%
You'll see it doesn't come up.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:21.440 align:middle line:90%
So it's enough.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:26.620 align:middle line:84%
The problem is, to get this,
the local assignment, I can get.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:29.150 align:middle line:84%
The problem why,
this implies global.

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:30.380 align:middle line:90%
So we'll see that.

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:33.320 align:middle line:84%
But the semi-- it
seems counterintuitive.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:35.170 align:middle line:90%
But actually, it's enough.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:36.295 align:middle line:90%
Was there another question?

00:19:36.295 --> 00:19:37.253 align:middle line:90%
Do you have a question?

00:19:37.253 --> 00:19:37.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you remind
me what the x star is?

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:40.870 align:middle line:90%
Is that the same--

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:42.203 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh, good, good, good.

00:19:42.203 --> 00:19:44.360 align:middle line:90%
This is any-- OK, so good.

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:45.100 align:middle line:90%
What is x star?

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:46.220 align:middle line:90%
No, sorry.

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:49.180 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:50.590 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:53.560 align:middle line:90%
Let me call it x tilde.

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.010 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:56.470 align:middle line:90%
Let me-- you're right.

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.840 align:middle line:90%
The extract-- OK.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:19:59.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:03.745 align:middle line:90%
So in p star outputs x tilde.

00:20:03.745 --> 00:20:06.050 align:middle line:84%
It's crooked, kind
of a cheating x.

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:07.870 align:middle line:84%
So let's use
crooked, like tilde,

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.160 align:middle line:84%
to denote a crooked
cheating thing.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:16.210 align:middle line:84%
And he's also going to output
some tilde because his is also

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:16.970 align:middle line:90%
crooked.

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:21.190 align:middle line:84%
And we want to argue
that this crooked x is

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:26.500 align:middle line:84%
indistinguishable from the
crooked x given by the cheating

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:27.080 align:middle line:90%
prover.

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.783 align:middle line:90%
We're going to-- yeah.

00:20:28.783 --> 00:20:30.700 align:middle line:84%
Oh, yeah, so I need to
say that x was-- sorry.

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:31.660 align:middle line:90%
So good.

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:36.300 align:middle line:84%
So let's say, again, the local
assignment generator, it outputs

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:40.020 align:middle line:90%
a crooked x with Partial.

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:41.490 align:middle line:84%
Assignments to
some of the wires,

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:49.600 align:middle line:84%
to the L wires such that it's
consistent, nonsignaling,

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:51.330 align:middle line:90%
and it's also non-- and--

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:57.450 align:middle line:84%
sorry, and x tilde
is indistinguishable

00:20:57.450 --> 00:20:59.940 align:middle line:90%
from the x tilde of p star.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.930 align:middle line:90%
So p star also outputs x tilde.

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:04.890 align:middle line:90%
They look the same.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.650 align:middle line:84%
Note, if it was nonadaptive,
it would be fixed.

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:12.090 align:middle line:84%
So then x tilde is
fixed ahead of time.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:13.840 align:middle line:84%
Nobody will need to
generate them anymore.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:14.460 align:middle line:90%
It's fixed.

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:18.150 align:middle line:84%
And the extractor, the
local assignment generator,

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:22.290 align:middle line:84%
will only give
values to the wires.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:27.850 align:middle line:84%
But in the adaptive setting,
the p star, you give him CRS,

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:29.500 align:middle line:90%
he gives you a different x star.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:30.660 align:middle line:90%
The x tilde.

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:31.730 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:34.230 align:middle line:84%
He gives you a
different x tilde.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:38.060 align:middle line:84%
So every time he gives
you a local window,

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:44.120 align:middle line:84%
it will be with respect to a
different x tilde, possibly,

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:45.140 align:middle line:90%
in the adaptive setting.

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:46.765 align:middle line:84%
In the nonadaptive
setting, it's always

00:21:46.765 --> 00:21:50.720 align:middle line:84%
going to be with
the same x tilde.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:52.520 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:53.476 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:21:53.476 --> 00:21:56.630 align:middle line:90%


00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:58.160 align:middle line:84%
Let's try to see
why we get local--

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:01.680 align:middle line:84%
how we construct this
local assignment generator.

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:03.210 align:middle line:90%
So I have a cheating prover.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:06.710 align:middle line:90%
So fix, so proof.

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:16.820 align:middle line:84%
So fix p star fix
a poly size p star.

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:22.140 align:middle line:84%
I want to get a window to
the-- there's the circuit.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:27.830 align:middle line:84%
We have the circuit C that
has x, maybe a witness.

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:32.910 align:middle line:84%
I want to use the C, the
p, the cheating prover.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:34.360 align:middle line:90%
So he's going to give me--

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:35.610 align:middle line:90%
OK, so fixed p star.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.830 align:middle line:84%
For any CRS, he's
going to give me x.

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:41.520 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get from
him in x and a window

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:43.750 align:middle line:84%
to some of the wires
of the circuit.

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:45.280 align:middle line:90%
So let's see how.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:47.610 align:middle line:90%
So here's my extractor.

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:49.440 align:middle line:84%
The extractor or
the local assignment

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:52.170 align:middle line:84%
generator-- it's going to
use p star as an oracle.

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:54.390 align:middle line:84%
He's going to make
use of p star.

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:57.210 align:middle line:90%
He's going to get i1 up to il.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:58.270 align:middle line:90%
What does he do?

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.590 align:middle line:84%
He wants to get the
values of these wires.

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:03.070 align:middle line:90%
What does he do?

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:05.230 align:middle line:90%
He's going to run p star.

00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:08.350 align:middle line:84%
He's going to run p star
on a CRS that he chooses.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:14.130 align:middle line:90%
So he's going to generate CRS.

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:15.990 align:middle line:90%
We'll see how in a second.

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:19.470 align:middle line:84%
And then he's going to
run p star on the CRS

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:22.510 align:middle line:90%
to get a value and a SNARG.

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:28.710 align:middle line:84%
And then he's going to extract
the wires from this hash value.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:29.920 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:31.990 align:middle line:90%
So how does he generate the CRS?

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:39.540 align:middle line:84%
CRS is going to be a pi for
CRS BARG and a hash key.

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:46.470 align:middle line:84%
So he's going to
generate hash key

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:51.490 align:middle line:84%
by running gen and these
location, i1 up to il.

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:54.810 align:middle line:84%
So let me actually-- he's
going to first generate

00:23:54.810 --> 00:24:05.010 align:middle line:84%
hash key, and trapdoor, and gen
some extractable hash with 1

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:10.900 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda number of wires
in the circuit and i1 up to il.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:14.430 align:middle line:84%
These are the wires
he wants to extract

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:16.420 align:middle line:90%
because he has i1 up to il.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:19.050 align:middle line:84%
He wants to extract the
values of these wires.

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:21.060 align:middle line:84%
So he's going to give
you a hash key that's

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:24.210 align:middle line:90%
extractable on these locations.

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:29.080 align:middle line:84%
And then he's also
going to generate gen

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:35.530 align:middle line:90%
for the BARG to get CRA BARG.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:37.090 align:middle line:84%
He's just going to
do this honestly.

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:40.585 align:middle line:84%
Here, he has 1 to
the lambda k and n.

00:24:40.585 --> 00:24:44.380 align:middle line:90%


00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:45.250 align:middle line:90%
This is n prime.

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:49.090 align:middle line:84%
The n prime, he's going
to generate exactly as

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:50.180 align:middle line:90%
in the construction.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:52.970 align:middle line:90%
And then he runs.

00:24:52.970 --> 00:25:02.140 align:middle line:84%
Now, run p star with
hash key and CRS BARG.

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:04.260 align:middle line:90%
That's the CRS he gives p star.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.850 align:middle line:90%


00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:11.300 align:middle line:90%
And get v and pi BARG.

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:16.330 align:middle line:84%
That's what the
p star gives you.

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:21.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, if this is
rejected, start again.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:26.200 align:middle line:90%
So if pi BARG--

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:31.540 align:middle line:90%
or if v and pi BARG--

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:35.570 align:middle line:90%
so if the verifier with input--

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:36.350 align:middle line:90%
oh, wait.

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:36.850 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:38.480 align:middle line:90%
X star also, x tilde.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:39.560 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:42.440 align:middle line:90%
So the p star gives what?

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:44.320 align:middle line:84%
Some x tilde because
it's adaptive.

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:47.380 align:middle line:90%
He gives him some x and a proof.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:50.480 align:middle line:84%
The proof is a hash
value and the BARG.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:51.980 align:middle line:90%
That's what the proof is.

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:53.080 align:middle line:90%
Now he checks.

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:55.810 align:middle line:84%
If v-- if the verifier
rejects this proof--

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:57.230 align:middle line:90%
if it's not a good proof--

00:25:57.230 --> 00:26:00.880 align:middle line:90%
so if v, given CRS--

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:01.970 align:middle line:90%
which is these two.

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:03.550 align:middle line:90%
This is the CRS--

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:07.600 align:middle line:84%
given CRS, given x
tilde, and given pi--

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:14.235 align:middle line:84%
this is pi-- outputs ,
if it rejects, try again.

00:26:14.235 --> 00:26:19.030 align:middle line:90%


00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:20.960 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by try again?

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:25.560 align:middle line:84%
Again, generate a hash
key, generate a BARG, run.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:27.850 align:middle line:84%
If this is accepted--
if it's rejected,

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:34.150 align:middle line:84%
try again until you
get something good.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:39.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, OK-- until we
get something good.

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:42.670 align:middle line:84%
Once you get something
good, you're happy.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:44.320 align:middle line:90%
But then, what do you do?

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:45.940 align:middle line:90%
You output.

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:47.490 align:middle line:90%
So try again.

00:26:47.490 --> 00:27:00.510 align:middle line:84%
If success, then
output the x tilde.

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:02.610 align:middle line:90%
And what wire values?

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:04.030 align:middle line:90%
Extract from the v.

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:07.680 align:middle line:84%
So remember, this V. Is hash of
all the wires of the circuit.

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:08.770 align:middle line:90%
Extract.

00:27:08.770 --> 00:27:11.200 align:middle line:84%
You'll get the wires
corresponding to i1 up to il.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:21.530 align:middle line:84%
So output this, and output
extract with trapdoor the value

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:26.450 align:middle line:90%
v to get wi1 up to wil.

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:27.830 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:30.780 align:middle line:84%
That's what the extractor-- the
local assignment generator does.

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.030 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Why can you do this?

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:34.470 align:middle line:90%
The slide was adaptive.

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:37.810 align:middle line:84%
So if you change the CRS,
why can you fix the x?

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:39.060 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: No, it's not fixed.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:39.602 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:27:39.602 --> 00:27:40.940 align:middle line:90%
So what do I do?

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:44.270 align:middle line:90%
I generate hash key and a BARG.

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:45.030 align:middle line:90%
I run him.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:47.630 align:middle line:90%
He gives me x tilde.

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:50.790 align:middle line:84%
If I'm happy with this
proof, I output x tilde.

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:52.740 align:middle line:90%
The x tilde depends on the CRS.

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:58.035 align:middle line:84%
So given i1 to il, I'm going to
get a different x tilde for i1

00:27:58.035 --> 00:27:58.610 align:middle line:90%
up to il.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:05.810 align:middle line:84%
So every time I get i1 to il,
I generate a CRS for my SNARG.

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:07.020 align:middle line:90%
I run the prover.

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:09.870 align:middle line:90%
I get x tilde and a proof.

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:12.830 align:middle line:84%
If I'm happy with the
proof, I output x tilde

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:14.043 align:middle line:90%
and extracted wires.

00:28:14.043 --> 00:28:15.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So these
are just wire values.

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:16.220 align:middle line:90%
They're not witnesses.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:16.887 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:28:16.887 --> 00:28:17.890 align:middle line:90%
No, they're wire value.

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:18.390 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:20.580 align:middle line:90%
These are not witnesses.

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:24.740 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I use W for what
wires, but it's also witness.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:27.613 align:middle line:90%


00:28:27.613 --> 00:28:28.113 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:28:28.113 --> 00:28:31.362 align:middle line:90%


00:28:31.362 --> 00:28:33.160 align:middle line:90%
A bit of abuse of notation.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:33.660 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:35.040 align:middle line:90%
These are wire values.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:36.100 align:middle line:90%
Forget about witness.

00:28:36.100 --> 00:28:37.990 align:middle line:84%
There's no witnesses
now, just wires.

00:28:37.990 --> 00:28:38.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:39.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:40.967 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you like
rejection sampling

00:28:40.967 --> 00:28:42.930 align:middle line:84%
until you get the
verifier to accept, then

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:45.090 align:middle line:84%
how would the distribution
of x going to be

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:46.560 align:middle line:90%
the distribution of p star?

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:48.060 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:49.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:49.630 align:middle line:90%
That's good.

00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:51.564 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to answer.

00:28:51.564 --> 00:28:52.064 align:middle line:90%
Did you--

00:28:52.064 --> 00:28:53.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's a
different question.

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:53.550 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: A different question.

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:53.800 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:55.217 align:middle line:84%
Let me first answer
this question.

00:28:55.217 --> 00:28:58.680 align:middle line:90%


00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:01.300 align:middle line:84%
The question was-- first,
there's a couple of issues.

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:04.260 align:middle line:84%
First of all, how many times
am I going to do this rejection

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:08.280 align:middle line:84%
sampling/ Am I going to run
forever What's going on here?

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:09.485 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is no.

00:29:09.485 --> 00:29:11.110 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I'm not
going to run forever.

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.440 align:middle line:84%
So fix a p star
that, let's say, is

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:25.810 align:middle line:84%
accepted with non-negligible
probability epsilon.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:27.820 align:middle line:90%
Epsilon is non-negligible.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:29.710 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to try again.

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:38.520 align:middle line:84%
At most, I know poly in lambda
and 1 over epsilon times.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:42.910 align:middle line:90%


00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:46.390 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to do this only
polynomially many times.

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:49.270 align:middle line:84%
Think of epsilon as
1 over poly lambda.

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:52.450 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to run in
time poly in lambda.

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:54.226 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:54.226 --> 00:29:56.710 align:middle line:90%
Now, given that-- and OK.

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:58.420 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing
I want to argue

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:01.030 align:middle line:90%
is the probability that I will--

00:30:01.030 --> 00:30:03.490 align:middle line:84%
and so I'm going to run
the most poly lambda in 1

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:04.250 align:middle line:90%
over epsilon time.

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:05.030 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:07.660 align:middle line:84%
And you say, what if
all of them are reject?

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:11.230 align:middle line:84%
Then I'm going to say,
OK, I was defeated.

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:14.000 align:middle line:84%
What is the probability
that I'll be defeated?

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:17.410 align:middle line:84%
Negligible because each
time I'm starting fresh

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:21.676 align:middle line:84%
and the probability
of defeat is only--

00:30:21.676 --> 00:30:24.050 align:middle line:90%
is 1 over--

00:30:24.050 --> 00:30:28.690 align:middle line:90%
OK, I'm accepted.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:32.650 align:middle line:84%
OK, I'm accepted with
probability epsilon.

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:36.950 align:middle line:84%
If I repeat it lambda
over epsilon times,

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:39.620 align:middle line:84%
by just concentration
bound, by Chernoff bound,

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:41.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be
accepted in one of them

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:43.220 align:middle line:84%
with very, very
high probability--

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:46.390 align:middle line:84%
exponential, like,
exponential in 2

00:30:46.390 --> 00:30:48.620 align:middle line:84%
to the minus lambda or
something like that.

00:30:48.620 --> 00:30:52.720 align:middle line:84%
So if I repeat it enough
times, one of them,

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:55.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to the
probability of BARG of me

00:30:55.510 --> 00:30:59.716 align:middle line:90%
being defeated is negligible.

00:30:59.716 --> 00:31:02.480 align:middle line:84%
So but now, going
back to your question,

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:05.500 align:middle line:84%
the question is,
how do I know that x

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:09.280 align:middle line:84%
tilde here has the same
distribution as the x tilde of p

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:10.420 align:middle line:90%
star?

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:11.690 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's the question.

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:14.770 align:middle line:90%
So good.

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:19.140 align:middle line:90%
So actually, I was a bit sloppy.

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.890 align:middle line:84%
When I say x tilde is
in the signal from this,

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:25.500 align:middle line:84%
I mean, in this thing for x
tilde condition on accept.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:26.630 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, yeah.

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:33.710 align:middle line:84%
This is condition
on p star accepting.

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:36.650 align:middle line:84%
So I can assume
without loss of-- yeah.

00:31:36.650 --> 00:31:39.720 align:middle line:84%
So let's think of p star
either as outputting

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:42.380 align:middle line:84%
but there's no point in
him giving a bad proof.

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:43.560 align:middle line:90%
He's going to be rejected.

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:47.315 align:middle line:84%
Either he's defeat or
giving x with a good proof.

00:31:47.315 --> 00:31:49.880 align:middle line:90%


00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:54.898 align:middle line:84%
So I always output x tilde with
probability 1 minus negligible.

00:31:54.898 --> 00:31:56.690 align:middle line:84%
And this x tilde is
going to be distributed

00:31:56.690 --> 00:32:01.470 align:middle line:84%
like x tilde conditioned on
the p star not a boarding.

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:02.180 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:03.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So then
those two distributions

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.900 align:middle line:84%
are actually identical right
up to the slight probability

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:07.730 align:middle line:90%
that you give up at the end?

00:32:07.730 --> 00:32:09.080 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: No.

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:11.990 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the question was,
is the x tilde of p star

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:15.720 align:middle line:84%
actually identical to the
x tilde of the extractor?

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:17.750 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is not
quite because the x tilde

00:32:17.750 --> 00:32:22.580 align:middle line:84%
of the extractor runs gen
with these specific i's.

00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:26.390 align:middle line:84%
And x tilde is run with p
star with, let's say, 1, 2, 3,

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:29.150 align:middle line:84%
up to l, I don't
know, like, fixed.

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:31.080 align:middle line:90%
And the I's change.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:32.220 align:middle line:90%
That's the only difference.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:33.950 align:middle line:84%
And that's why it's
indistinguishable because the p

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:34.860 align:middle line:90%
star doesn't know.

00:32:34.860 --> 00:32:38.990 align:middle line:84%
Because of the
index hiding, p star

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:42.620 align:middle line:84%
has no idea what the
index-- which indices

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:44.880 align:middle line:90%
are sitting behind the hash key.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:48.480 align:middle line:84%
So the x tilde he
gives is the same.

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:50.000 align:middle line:90%
It's indistinguishable.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:51.860 align:middle line:90%
Nikon, did you-- are you--

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:55.267 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I just to make
sure from the last point.

00:32:55.267 --> 00:32:57.350 align:middle line:84%
Each trial here will work
with probability epsilon

00:32:57.350 --> 00:32:59.910 align:middle line:84%
minus negligible because
of the index hiding, right?

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:00.780 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:01.460 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:03.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you're right.

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:03.950 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:05.600 align:middle line:84%
Epsilon minus--
that's why the epsilon

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:08.580 align:middle line:84%
has to be non-negligible,
because you lose negligible.

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:13.920 align:middle line:84%
And it needs to be negligible
compared to the epsilon.

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:16.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So my question
was, the extract algorithm

00:33:16.170 --> 00:33:18.600 align:middle line:84%
for the SEH is only
required to extract

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:22.680 align:middle line:90%
when V is an eval of x, right?

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:25.260 align:middle line:84%
Is that true, or
is that not true?

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:26.130 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:33:26.130 --> 00:33:26.843 align:middle line:90%
One second.

00:33:26.843 --> 00:33:32.010 align:middle line:90%


00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:33.810 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:35.850 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:36.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:37.500 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:40.260 align:middle line:90%
The completeness was assuming--

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:41.590 align:middle line:90%
holds.

00:33:41.590 --> 00:33:47.490 align:middle line:84%
But I didn't write
it actually down.

