00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:07.350 align:middle line:90%
actually, yeah?

00:47:07.350 --> 00:47:10.177 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is the i
[? changed ?] [INAUDIBLE]?

00:47:10.177 --> 00:47:10.760 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:11.390 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry.

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:12.600 align:middle line:90%
I changed-- yeah, sorry.

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:13.580 align:middle line:90%
This is J.

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:14.510 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:47:14.510 --> 00:47:15.420 align:middle line:90%
Sorry about that.

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:16.190 align:middle line:90%
This is-- yeah.

00:47:16.190 --> 00:47:20.220 align:middle line:84%
So I give you all-- this
is an index language.

00:47:20.220 --> 00:47:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So I give the instances
are-- oh, ah, sorry.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:24.590 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:47:24.590 --> 00:47:28.850 align:middle line:90%
These are all for all i.

00:47:28.850 --> 00:47:31.550 align:middle line:84%
Just J is a specific
J, so I don't want to--

00:47:31.550 --> 00:47:33.900 align:middle line:84%
J is the one that
I'm binding on.

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:38.430 align:middle line:84%
But I give you the
instances are--

00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:38.930 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:40.400 align:middle line:90%
All of them are i.

00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:41.630 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:44.630 align:middle line:84%
J is the specific one on
which I'm going to be--

00:47:44.630 --> 00:47:46.190 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to cheat on.

00:47:46.190 --> 00:47:47.160 align:middle line:90%
All of them are i.

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:48.440 align:middle line:90%
Sorry about the-- yeah.

00:47:48.440 --> 00:47:53.720 align:middle line:90%


00:47:53.720 --> 00:47:56.280 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:59.560 align:middle line:90%


00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:04.550 align:middle line:84%
So now the question is, now,
what about nonsignaling?

00:48:04.550 --> 00:48:07.800 align:middle line:84%
And nonsignaling-- I don't
want to go into a lot of depth.

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:12.320 align:middle line:90%
But let me be hand-wavy here.

00:48:12.320 --> 00:48:14.480 align:middle line:90%
The idea of why we're non--

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:19.610 align:middle line:84%
so we want to argue that the
local assignment generator--

00:48:19.610 --> 00:48:25.520 align:middle line:84%
the assignment values that he
gives for any subset of wires

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:30.300 align:middle line:84%
do not depend on the other
wires that is given to him.

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:34.880 align:middle line:84%
So if we look, if the extractor
is given a bunch of wires

00:48:34.880 --> 00:48:38.240 align:middle line:84%
and you look at the
assignment and a subset,

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:42.570 align:middle line:84%
the distribution of the
assignment that you get,

00:48:42.570 --> 00:48:45.680 align:middle line:84%
it looks the same no
matter what the other--

00:48:45.680 --> 00:48:48.680 align:middle line:90%
the rest of the wires are.

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:51.220 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:55.860 align:middle line:84%
It just follows
from the fact that--

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:58.080 align:middle line:90%
I just erased it here.

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:04.560 align:middle line:84%
So it follows from the fact that
when we do a sum or extractable

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:07.830 align:middle line:84%
hash with many--
when we construct

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:10.890 align:middle line:84%
a somewhat extractable
hash with many indices that

00:49:10.890 --> 00:49:12.850 align:middle line:84%
were extractable
on many indices,

00:49:12.850 --> 00:49:17.670 align:middle line:84%
we want to have the property
that even if we extract it

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:24.690 align:middle line:84%
on some indices, the
index hiding still

00:49:24.690 --> 00:49:25.840 align:middle line:90%
holds for the other.

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:27.900 align:middle line:84%
So in other words,
think of the way

00:49:27.900 --> 00:49:30.150 align:middle line:84%
we're going to construct the
somewhat extractable hash

00:49:30.150 --> 00:49:31.920 align:middle line:90%
is by repetition.

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:33.730 align:middle line:84%
We're going to repeat
it many, many times.

00:49:33.730 --> 00:49:40.680 align:middle line:84%
So even if you saw if I
extracted wire i1, wire i2, wire

00:49:40.680 --> 00:49:48.340 align:middle line:84%
i3, the indices behind the other
wires are completely hidden.

00:49:48.340 --> 00:49:53.730 align:middle line:84%
So here, when you
look at extract

00:49:53.730 --> 00:49:58.390 align:middle line:84%
and a subset of witnesses,
how do you find extract these?

00:49:58.390 --> 00:50:03.310 align:middle line:84%
How does the local assignment
generator generate these wires?

00:50:03.310 --> 00:50:06.720 align:middle line:84%
He generates these wires using
the corresponding trapdoors

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:08.740 align:middle line:90%
without the other trapdoors.

00:50:08.740 --> 00:50:11.103 align:middle line:90%
So he actually has no idea.

00:50:11.103 --> 00:50:13.890 align:middle line:90%


00:50:13.890 --> 00:50:17.970 align:middle line:84%
This should not signal
anything about the value

00:50:17.970 --> 00:50:19.680 align:middle line:90%
of the other wires.

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:22.470 align:middle line:90%
So again, let me say it again.

00:50:22.470 --> 00:50:24.700 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I didn't say
it quite right.

00:50:24.700 --> 00:50:28.170 align:middle line:84%
But the local
assignment generator,

00:50:28.170 --> 00:50:34.300 align:middle line:84%
when he runs the cheating
prover and the cheating prover,

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:36.630 align:middle line:84%
when you extract
from the cheating

00:50:36.630 --> 00:50:40.300 align:middle line:84%
prover the value of
some of the wires,

00:50:40.300 --> 00:50:45.060 align:middle line:84%
you need only to do that using
the trapdoors for these wires.

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:47.220 align:middle line:84%
The cheating prover
has no idea what

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:50.390 align:middle line:84%
the other wires-- what the trap
door of the other wires are.

00:50:50.390 --> 00:50:53.890 align:middle line:84%
So these values should
not depend at all

00:50:53.890 --> 00:50:59.900 align:middle line:84%
about the other indices used
in the other repetitions.

00:50:59.900 --> 00:51:05.040 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, you've broken the
timing for these repetitions.

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:12.640 align:middle line:84%
So the point is the nonsignaling
really follows in a sense

00:51:12.640 --> 00:51:16.820 align:middle line:84%
from the nonsignaling in the
somewhat extractable hash.

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:18.340 align:middle line:84%
The somewhat
extractable hash, when

00:51:18.340 --> 00:51:22.150 align:middle line:84%
we repeat, when we do
repetition, what we can argue

00:51:22.150 --> 00:51:28.660 align:middle line:84%
is any cheating prover
who, given a hash key,

00:51:28.660 --> 00:51:31.120 align:middle line:90%
generates a value.

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:37.570 align:middle line:84%
If you extract some of the
wires, the value you get

00:51:37.570 --> 00:51:40.820 align:middle line:84%
do not depend on
the other indices.

00:51:40.820 --> 00:51:44.300 align:middle line:84%
This is a property of the
somewhat extractable hash.

00:51:44.300 --> 00:51:47.380 align:middle line:84%
So nonsignaling here
follows directly

00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:50.920 align:middle line:84%
from the nonsignaling of the
somewhat extractable hash.

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:52.700 align:middle line:90%
So I'm running out of time.

00:51:52.700 --> 00:51:56.080 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to go too
much in depth because I

00:51:56.080 --> 00:51:57.560 align:middle line:90%
think it's not as interesting.

00:51:57.560 --> 00:52:02.860 align:middle line:84%
I want to actually go and talk
about why is this not sufficient

00:52:02.860 --> 00:52:04.640 align:middle line:90%
and when is it sufficient.

00:52:04.640 --> 00:52:07.465 align:middle line:90%


00:52:07.465 --> 00:52:09.050 align:middle line:90%
So let's call-- let's say--

00:52:09.050 --> 00:52:10.030 align:middle line:90%
OK, so we have--

00:52:10.030 --> 00:52:12.610 align:middle line:84%
suppose we have a
cheating prover.

00:52:12.610 --> 00:52:17.680 align:middle line:84%
We manage to construct from him
this local assignment generator.

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:20.350 align:middle line:90%
So we have the circuit.

00:52:20.350 --> 00:52:22.550 align:middle line:90%
We have a cheating prover.

00:52:22.550 --> 00:52:24.280 align:middle line:90%
He gave me an x.

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:25.115 align:middle line:90%
We want to argue.

00:52:25.115 --> 00:52:25.990 align:middle line:90%
It's in the language.

00:52:25.990 --> 00:52:28.550 align:middle line:84%
If he convinced me, it
must be in the language.

00:52:28.550 --> 00:52:30.250 align:middle line:90%
That's what soundness means.

00:52:30.250 --> 00:52:31.430 align:middle line:90%
What do we know?

00:52:31.430 --> 00:52:34.755 align:middle line:84%
We know when we look at any
window, everything looks good.

00:52:34.755 --> 00:52:37.130 align:middle line:84%
And when you look at the window
that contains the output,

00:52:37.130 --> 00:52:39.700 align:middle line:90%
it's 1.

00:52:39.700 --> 00:52:44.337 align:middle line:84%
Does that mean we can
have an entire assignment

00:52:44.337 --> 00:52:46.920 align:middle line:84%
to all the wires that everything
is consistent, in which case,

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:50.180 align:middle line:90%
we can then sound?

00:52:50.180 --> 00:52:52.240 align:middle line:84%
So can we piece these
things together?

00:52:52.240 --> 00:53:02.450 align:middle line:90%


00:53:02.450 --> 00:53:06.110 align:middle line:90%
So the answer is yes and no.

00:53:06.110 --> 00:53:06.750 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:53:06.750 --> 00:53:09.925 align:middle line:84%
So in general for
np, the answer is no.

00:53:09.925 --> 00:53:11.550 align:middle line:84%
We don't know how to
piece it together.

00:53:11.550 --> 00:53:14.540 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, we believe that
you can't piece it together

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:16.460 align:middle line:90%
for any np language.

00:53:16.460 --> 00:53:24.470 align:middle line:84%
We believe that there exists
very hard NP languages

00:53:24.470 --> 00:53:26.610 align:middle line:84%
that there's no way you
can piece it together.

00:53:26.610 --> 00:53:29.480 align:middle line:90%
But let me tell you why.

00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:32.330 align:middle line:84%
Why is it hard to
piece things together?

00:53:32.330 --> 00:53:34.820 align:middle line:90%
And the reason is, even--

00:53:34.820 --> 00:53:37.230 align:middle line:84%
let's even think,
now, for simplicity,

00:53:37.230 --> 00:53:38.940 align:middle line:84%
I think, it would
be easier to think,

00:53:38.940 --> 00:53:43.890 align:middle line:84%
let's say, even in the
nonadaptive version, x is fixed.

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:49.500 align:middle line:84%
Can we argue that
for this fixed x,

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:53.250 align:middle line:84%
we can piece
everything together?

00:53:53.250 --> 00:53:55.980 align:middle line:84%
And the reason why it's hard
to piece everything together

00:53:55.980 --> 00:54:00.090 align:middle line:84%
is because this local
assignment generator, every time

00:54:00.090 --> 00:54:03.630 align:middle line:84%
you give them a
bunch of wires, think

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:07.620 align:middle line:90%
of even a valid kind of prover.

00:54:07.620 --> 00:54:08.680 align:middle line:90%
Think of a valid prover.

00:54:08.680 --> 00:54:11.860 align:middle line:84%
Every time you run him, he
uses a different witnesses.

00:54:11.860 --> 00:54:14.350 align:middle line:84%
Maybe he has many, many
witnesses in his disposal.

00:54:14.350 --> 00:54:16.240 align:middle line:84%
Every time you run
him, he's honest,

00:54:16.240 --> 00:54:18.165 align:middle line:90%
but with a different witness.

00:54:18.165 --> 00:54:19.540 align:middle line:84%
So it's actually
in the language.

00:54:19.540 --> 00:54:23.220 align:middle line:84%
But the question is, are we
able to construct a satisfying

00:54:23.220 --> 00:54:24.280 align:middle line:90%
assignment?

00:54:24.280 --> 00:54:26.760 align:middle line:84%
And the reason why this
is hard is-- or seems

00:54:26.760 --> 00:54:31.870 align:middle line:84%
hard is because, OK, we have
our local assignment generator.

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:33.760 align:middle line:90%
We give him a bunch of wires.

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:35.820 align:middle line:84%
He chooses one of his
witnesses at random--

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:37.890 align:middle line:84%
because that's what the cheating
prover does-- and gives him

00:54:37.890 --> 00:54:39.520 align:middle line:84%
an assignment with
this, the witness.

00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:40.960 align:middle line:90%
OK, local consistent.

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:41.470 align:middle line:90%
We're fine.

00:54:41.470 --> 00:54:42.340 align:middle line:90%
We're happy.

00:54:42.340 --> 00:54:43.600 align:middle line:90%
Now we run him again.

00:54:43.600 --> 00:54:45.360 align:middle line:90%
He uses a different witness.

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:46.295 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't match.

00:54:46.295 --> 00:54:49.350 align:middle line:90%


00:54:49.350 --> 00:54:51.130 align:middle line:90%
How do we match things together?

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:53.620 align:middle line:84%
So that's why in the
nondeterministic case

00:54:53.620 --> 00:54:57.400 align:middle line:84%
things are really difficult.
And we do have some results,

00:54:57.400 --> 00:54:58.960 align:middle line:90%
but for very specific languages.

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:01.110 align:middle line:90%
Let me not go into that.

00:55:01.110 --> 00:55:02.890 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk about
deterministic languages.

00:55:02.890 --> 00:55:05.260 align:middle line:90%
That seems to be much easier.

00:55:05.260 --> 00:55:08.530 align:middle line:84%
So let me first convince
you that we can do it.

00:55:08.530 --> 00:55:11.040 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll show
you where I cheated.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:16.440 align:middle line:90%
So let's first take a circuit.

00:55:16.440 --> 00:55:18.720 align:middle line:84%
We have a local
assignment generator.

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:21.970 align:middle line:84%
So let me convince you
that the output must be 1.

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:24.995 align:middle line:84%
So we have an
input-- x1 up to xn.

00:55:24.995 --> 00:55:28.500 align:middle line:90%


00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:31.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to call my local
assignment generator.

00:55:31.180 --> 00:55:34.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to tell him,
give me the values here.

00:55:34.260 --> 00:55:35.070 align:middle line:90%
Or here, let me--

00:55:35.070 --> 00:55:36.010 align:middle line:90%
I have l, right?

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:37.740 align:middle line:84%
So let me use my l
here, here, and here.

00:55:37.740 --> 00:55:38.640 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:40.000 align:middle line:90%
He gives me values.

00:55:40.000 --> 00:55:42.910 align:middle line:84%
Now, this must be
consistent because I

00:55:42.910 --> 00:55:46.040 align:middle line:84%
know local consistency holds
probability 1 minus negligible.

00:55:46.040 --> 00:55:49.630 align:middle line:84%
And all of them are
consistent, which means

00:55:49.630 --> 00:55:51.230 align:middle line:90%
these are the correct values.

00:55:51.230 --> 00:55:53.320 align:middle line:90%
Now we're deterministic.

00:55:53.320 --> 00:55:56.260 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about a
deterministic circuit.

00:55:56.260 --> 00:55:58.292 align:middle line:84%
So if this is
locally consistent,

00:55:58.292 --> 00:55:59.750 align:middle line:84%
it means this is
the correct value.

00:55:59.750 --> 00:56:00.050 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:56:00.050 --> 00:56:01.883 align:middle line:84%
If it's an AND, it's
an AND, whatever it is.

00:56:01.883 --> 00:56:04.780 align:middle line:90%
So all of these are correct.

00:56:04.780 --> 00:56:08.200 align:middle line:84%
I can argue, when I run the
cheating-- the assignment-- so

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:12.910 align:middle line:84%
I want to argue essentially
any local assignment

00:56:12.910 --> 00:56:16.423 align:middle line:84%
generator, every wire he gives
me must be the correct one.

00:56:16.423 --> 00:56:18.590 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm done because
the output is the correct.

00:56:18.590 --> 00:56:20.600 align:middle line:84%
And if he gives me
output 1, voila.

00:56:20.600 --> 00:56:21.230 align:middle line:90%
It's correct.

00:56:21.230 --> 00:56:22.750 align:middle line:90%
I'm done.

00:56:22.750 --> 00:56:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to argue,
kind of by induction,

00:56:25.450 --> 00:56:28.750 align:middle line:84%
that every wire he gives
me must be the correct one.

00:56:28.750 --> 00:56:30.070 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:56:30.070 --> 00:56:30.850 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:56:30.850 --> 00:56:34.130 align:middle line:84%
Let's try-- so I'm going
to argue layer by layer.

00:56:34.130 --> 00:56:37.270 align:middle line:84%
The induction is going to be
by the-- think of the circuit

00:56:37.270 --> 00:56:37.940 align:middle line:90%
as layered.

00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:41.590 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to argue this
by induction on the layers.

00:56:41.590 --> 00:56:44.390 align:middle line:84%
Layer 1-- what does the
cheating prover gives me?

00:56:44.390 --> 00:56:46.070 align:middle line:90%
He gives me an assignment.

00:56:46.070 --> 00:56:49.040 align:middle line:84%
Now, I know I proved
it locally satisfiable,

00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:51.290 align:middle line:84%
which means it
satisfies the gate.

00:56:51.290 --> 00:56:52.570 align:middle line:90%
So it's correct.

00:56:52.570 --> 00:56:54.910 align:middle line:90%
So layer 1, correct.

00:56:54.910 --> 00:56:56.770 align:middle line:90%
Let's go to layer 2.

00:56:56.770 --> 00:56:59.660 align:middle line:84%
Layer 2, now I'm
going to ask him this.

00:56:59.660 --> 00:57:02.200 align:middle line:84%
Let's say I'm going
to ask him these.

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:04.690 align:middle line:84%
I want to argue that
this is correct.

00:57:04.690 --> 00:57:07.810 align:middle line:84%
All I know is that it's
locally consistent.

00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:11.450 align:middle line:84%
But I know that
these are correct.

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:13.760 align:middle line:90%
Why are these correct?

00:57:13.760 --> 00:57:16.780 align:middle line:84%
Because of the
nonsignaling, I know.

00:57:16.780 --> 00:57:22.450 align:middle line:84%
If I asked, give me these
wires, give me these wires

00:57:22.450 --> 00:57:25.490 align:middle line:90%
with the input, it was correct.

00:57:25.490 --> 00:57:27.520 align:middle line:90%
I got the correct answer.

00:57:27.520 --> 00:57:31.370 align:middle line:84%
So now when I ask the extract
the local assignment generator,

00:57:31.370 --> 00:57:34.960 align:middle line:84%
give me these wires
with these, we

00:57:34.960 --> 00:57:38.660 align:middle line:84%
said the distribution is the
same up to-- or computationally

00:57:38.660 --> 00:57:39.950 align:middle line:90%
indistinguishable.

00:57:39.950 --> 00:57:45.920 align:middle line:84%
So when I asked him, give
me the value of this wire,

00:57:45.920 --> 00:57:49.550 align:middle line:84%
when I asked with
the inputs or asked,

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:53.245 align:middle line:84%
give me these wires with this,
it's the same distribution.

00:57:53.245 --> 00:57:55.800 align:middle line:84%
If it was correct
before, it's correct now.

00:57:55.800 --> 00:57:58.770 align:middle line:84%
So these are correct, and
this is locally consistent.

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:00.680 align:middle line:90%
So this is also correct.

00:58:00.680 --> 00:58:03.290 align:middle line:90%
So these layers are correct.

00:58:03.290 --> 00:58:06.180 align:middle line:84%
And again, now
let's look at these.

00:58:06.180 --> 00:58:07.170 align:middle line:90%
Is this correct?

00:58:07.170 --> 00:58:10.160 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, because these
still need to be correct

00:58:10.160 --> 00:58:11.630 align:middle line:90%
because we argued it correct.

00:58:11.630 --> 00:58:13.652 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't depend
on the distribution.

00:58:13.652 --> 00:58:15.860 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't depend on the--
every time I ask him this,

00:58:15.860 --> 00:58:18.360 align:middle line:84%
I get probability 1 minus
negligible-- so correct.

00:58:18.360 --> 00:58:20.660 align:middle line:90%
So this is correct.

00:58:20.660 --> 00:58:22.770 align:middle line:84%
By induction, the
output is correct.

00:58:22.770 --> 00:58:23.640 align:middle line:90%
I know it's 1.

00:58:23.640 --> 00:58:27.660 align:middle line:84%
That's what the local assignment
generator promised me.

00:58:27.660 --> 00:58:29.480 align:middle line:90%
So it's 1 and correct.

00:58:29.480 --> 00:58:30.280 align:middle line:90%
I'm done.

00:58:30.280 --> 00:58:36.545 align:middle line:90%


00:58:36.545 --> 00:58:37.410 align:middle line:90%
That's the proof.

00:58:37.410 --> 00:58:38.910 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, let me
tell you something.

00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:42.600 align:middle line:84%
When we wrote this
proof, we wrote it.

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:44.160 align:middle line:90%
We were happy with it.

00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:46.818 align:middle line:84%
We literally almost
submitted this thing.

00:58:46.818 --> 00:58:48.360 align:middle line:84%
A couple of days
before the deadline,

00:58:48.360 --> 00:58:49.880 align:middle line:90%
we noticed there's a bug.

00:58:49.880 --> 00:58:51.000 align:middle line:90%
This is wrong.

00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:51.865 align:middle line:90%
This does not work.

00:58:51.865 --> 00:58:54.403 align:middle line:90%


00:58:54.403 --> 00:58:56.820 align:middle line:84%
I'll tell you why it doesn't
work as opposed to asking you

00:58:56.820 --> 00:58:58.140 align:middle line:90%
because I think it's so subtle.

00:58:58.140 --> 00:58:59.970 align:middle line:84%
But does anybody see
why it doesn't work?

00:58:59.970 --> 00:59:01.762 align:middle line:84%
It's very, very hard
to see, in my opinion.

00:59:01.762 --> 00:59:03.510 align:middle line:84%
But I mean, clearly,
I didn't see it.

00:59:03.510 --> 00:59:08.600 align:middle line:84%
But anybody see
why I cheated here?

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:09.608 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:59:09.608 --> 00:59:11.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it
because we restricted

00:59:11.150 --> 00:59:12.850 align:middle line:90%
to deterministic witness?

00:59:12.850 --> 00:59:16.303 align:middle line:90%


00:59:16.303 --> 00:59:16.970 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:59:16.970 --> 00:59:17.670 align:middle line:90%
So what?

00:59:17.670 --> 00:59:18.260 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:59:18.260 --> 00:59:19.743 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, there's only the input.

00:59:19.743 --> 00:59:21.660 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the fact that
there's deterministic,

00:59:21.660 --> 00:59:25.077 align:middle line:84%
it's helpful for us because now
there's a notion of correctness.

00:59:25.077 --> 00:59:27.660 align:middle line:84%
Once there's a witness, there's
no-- what do you mean correct?

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:28.618 align:middle line:90%
Depends on the witness.

00:59:28.618 --> 00:59:31.140 align:middle line:84%
Every wire has, like,
if you use this witness,

00:59:31.140 --> 00:59:32.180 align:middle line:90%
it can be this value.

00:59:32.180 --> 00:59:34.500 align:middle line:84%
Here, because it's
deterministic,

00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:36.550 align:middle line:84%
there's only one
value for each wire.

00:59:36.550 --> 00:59:39.613 align:middle line:84%
And so there's the
notion of correct value.

00:59:39.613 --> 00:59:42.150 align:middle line:90%


00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:44.795 align:middle line:84%
And I can argue by induction
that everything is correct.

00:59:44.795 --> 00:59:55.830 align:middle line:90%


00:59:55.830 --> 01:00:00.768 align:middle line:90%
So I'll tell you why.

01:00:00.768 --> 01:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
I suffered through this.

01:00:01.960 --> 01:00:05.700 align:middle line:90%
I have the desire to have you--

01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:07.540 align:middle line:84%
to torture you a
little bit to see why.

01:00:07.540 --> 01:00:08.527 align:middle line:90%
And so like I--

01:00:08.527 --> 01:00:09.610 align:middle line:90%
like the experience I had.

01:00:09.610 --> 01:00:14.560 align:middle line:84%
But given that you only have
10 minutes left, I'll tell you.

01:00:14.560 --> 01:00:15.725 align:middle line:90%
So here's why.

01:00:15.725 --> 01:00:18.540 align:middle line:90%


01:00:18.540 --> 01:00:25.140 align:middle line:90%
So local consistency holds--

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:31.920 align:middle line:84%
or yeah, local consistency
holds with probability--

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:35.640 align:middle line:84%
or the nonsignaling, everything
holds with probability 1

01:00:35.640 --> 01:00:37.990 align:middle line:84%
minus negligible, not
with probability 1.

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:39.840 align:middle line:84%
If everything with
probability 1--

01:00:39.840 --> 01:00:44.980 align:middle line:84%
1, local consistency--
then this consists only 1.

01:00:44.980 --> 01:00:47.890 align:middle line:90%
Then, everything would work out.

01:00:47.890 --> 01:00:51.000 align:middle line:84%
If I promised you local
consistency with probability 1

01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:52.860 align:middle line:84%
and nonsignaling
with probability 1,

01:00:52.860 --> 01:00:55.390 align:middle line:84%
like it's exactly
the same, we're good.

01:00:55.390 --> 01:00:57.270 align:middle line:90%
Then I can prove.

01:00:57.270 --> 01:01:00.330 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's used in cryptography,
oh, come on, negligible.

01:01:00.330 --> 01:01:02.070 align:middle line:90%
It's 0.

01:01:02.070 --> 01:01:03.160 align:middle line:90%
Here, It's not zero.

01:01:03.160 --> 01:01:05.380 align:middle line:84%
It turns out,
negligible is a pain.

01:01:05.380 --> 01:01:07.540 align:middle line:84%
This is the entire
problem, the negligible.

01:01:07.540 --> 01:01:08.710 align:middle line:90%
And I'll tell you why.

01:01:08.710 --> 01:01:10.140 align:middle line:90%
It seems weird, but here's why.

01:01:10.140 --> 01:01:14.880 align:middle line:84%
The reason is this
is correct with

01:01:14.880 --> 01:01:16.570 align:middle line:90%
all-but-negligible probability.

01:01:16.570 --> 01:01:20.820 align:middle line:84%
So this is correct,
except probability mu.

01:01:20.820 --> 01:01:25.290 align:middle line:84%
This is correct, except
with probability mu.

01:01:25.290 --> 01:01:29.800 align:middle line:84%
But now this is correct if
this needs to be correct,

01:01:29.800 --> 01:01:31.140 align:middle line:90%
and this needs to be correct.

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:34.420 align:middle line:84%
If one of them is incorrect,
you're not correct.

01:01:34.420 --> 01:01:35.840 align:middle line:90%
So this is correct.

01:01:35.840 --> 01:01:41.080 align:middle line:84%
Is probably correct,
except probability 2 mu.

01:01:41.080 --> 01:01:43.400 align:middle line:90%
So here, you have 2 mu.

01:01:43.400 --> 01:01:47.410 align:middle line:90%
Now, 2 mu is also negligible.

01:01:47.410 --> 01:01:49.970 align:middle line:90%
This is also acceptable 2 mu.

01:01:49.970 --> 01:01:53.500 align:middle line:84%
But this-- for this to
be correct, both of them

01:01:53.500 --> 01:01:54.440 align:middle line:90%
need to be correct.

01:01:54.440 --> 01:01:58.720 align:middle line:84%
So here, you have
4 mu and then 8 mu.

01:01:58.720 --> 01:02:02.590 align:middle line:84%
You can get 2 to
the depth times mu.

01:02:02.590 --> 01:02:07.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, if the depth
is more than log,

01:02:07.480 --> 01:02:09.620 align:middle line:90%
it's not negligible anymore.

01:02:09.620 --> 01:02:13.120 align:middle line:90%


01:02:13.120 --> 01:02:16.120 align:middle line:84%
So the problem is 2 to the
depth may be much bigger than 1

01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:19.090 align:middle line:90%
over mu.

01:02:19.090 --> 01:02:24.820 align:middle line:84%
So there's this
exponential curse of--

01:02:24.820 --> 01:02:29.470 align:middle line:84%
we carry-- the error grows
by a factor of 2 each time

01:02:29.470 --> 01:02:32.590 align:middle line:90%
and eventually it can kill us.

01:02:32.590 --> 01:02:34.270 align:middle line:90%
That's the bug.

01:02:34.270 --> 01:02:36.650 align:middle line:84%
So this works if
it's bound to depth.

01:02:36.650 --> 01:02:37.970 align:middle line:90%
Log depth, yes.

01:02:37.970 --> 01:02:43.420 align:middle line:90%
More than that, not clear.

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:45.550 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:02:45.550 --> 01:02:47.590 align:middle line:90%
So what do we do?

01:02:47.590 --> 01:02:53.200 align:middle line:84%
So to fix this, there
is two ways to fix this.

01:02:53.200 --> 01:02:55.660 align:middle line:84%
Option number 1, which
is what we did originally

01:02:55.660 --> 01:02:58.760 align:middle line:84%
because we didn't have a better
idea, is we said, you know what?

01:02:58.760 --> 01:03:02.320 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that this
circuit is very narrow.

01:03:02.320 --> 01:03:06.730 align:middle line:84%
Think of bounded depth
deterministic computations--

01:03:06.730 --> 01:03:08.390 align:middle line:90%
sorry, bounded space.

01:03:08.390 --> 01:03:10.090 align:middle line:84%
So think of a
distributed computation

01:03:10.090 --> 01:03:12.500 align:middle line:84%
where each configuration
is very small--

01:03:12.500 --> 01:03:17.470 align:middle line:84%
so like a Turing
machine that has very--

01:03:17.470 --> 01:03:20.050 align:middle line:84%
the tableau is
very thin and long.

01:03:20.050 --> 01:03:21.680 align:middle line:90%
And then we said, you know what?

01:03:21.680 --> 01:03:26.960 align:middle line:84%
Let's make L, like where you're
statistically bounded on--

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:28.880 align:middle line:84%
let's make it an
entire configuration.

01:03:28.880 --> 01:03:31.715 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, you pay
with an entire--

01:03:31.715 --> 01:03:32.880 align:middle line:90%
with a space.

01:03:32.880 --> 01:03:35.880 align:middle line:84%
The SNARG, the proof
will grow with the space.

01:03:35.880 --> 01:03:38.430 align:middle line:84%
But now we say, you can
read the entire thing.

01:03:38.430 --> 01:03:41.940 align:middle line:84%
You can read-- so
here's the tableau.

01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:43.830 align:middle line:84%
You can read the
entire computation.

01:03:43.830 --> 01:03:45.660 align:middle line:84%
You can read the
entire configuration.

01:03:45.660 --> 01:03:48.600 align:middle line:84%
And now we say, the first
configuration is correct,

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:49.640 align:middle line:90%
so the probability mu.

01:03:49.640 --> 01:03:52.910 align:middle line:84%
The second configuration,
if this was correct,

01:03:52.910 --> 01:03:55.740 align:middle line:90%
this is 2 mu, 3 mu, 4 mu.

01:03:55.740 --> 01:03:57.800 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't grow
exponential anymore

01:03:57.800 --> 01:04:00.140 align:middle line:84%
because you read
the entire thing.

01:04:00.140 --> 01:04:03.450 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to read these
two configurations together.

01:04:03.450 --> 01:04:05.695 align:middle line:84%
If this was consistent,
this is consistent.

01:04:05.695 --> 01:04:08.070 align:middle line:84%
There's always a probability
of error that I'm incurring.

01:04:08.070 --> 01:04:09.380 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

01:04:09.380 --> 01:04:12.720 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't double because
I'm reading the entire thing.

01:04:12.720 --> 01:04:15.950 align:middle line:84%
I said, if this was a
correct configuration,

01:04:15.950 --> 01:04:18.140 align:middle line:84%
then when I read
this with this, this

01:04:18.140 --> 01:04:19.920 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
correct configuration.

01:04:19.920 --> 01:04:23.960 align:middle line:84%
But I read the entire thing,
so I don't double up things.

01:04:23.960 --> 01:04:29.660 align:middle line:84%
So we get SNARG for
any computation that's

01:04:29.660 --> 01:04:34.370 align:middle line:84%
bounded space where the
SNARG grows with the space.

01:04:34.370 --> 01:04:34.987 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:34.987 --> 01:04:36.570 align:middle line:84%
But let me tell you,
we can do better.

01:04:36.570 --> 01:04:39.170 align:middle line:84%
We can actually-- we don't
need to grow with the space.

01:04:39.170 --> 01:04:41.810 align:middle line:84%
So I'll just say a word because
I'm running out of time,

01:04:41.810 --> 01:04:43.860 align:middle line:84%
and I want to do
a quick summary.

01:04:43.860 --> 01:04:49.910 align:middle line:84%
But the idea of not running
into this exponential curse idea

01:04:49.910 --> 01:04:51.860 align:middle line:90%
is to say the following.

01:04:51.860 --> 01:04:55.850 align:middle line:90%
Let's do this hash in the BARG.

01:04:55.850 --> 01:04:57.110 align:middle line:90%
So what did we do?

01:04:57.110 --> 01:05:00.290 align:middle line:84%
This SNARG is like, hash
all the wires and then BARG.

01:05:00.290 --> 01:05:03.950 align:middle line:84%
The idea is don't do hash and
BARG in the original circuit.

01:05:03.950 --> 01:05:07.280 align:middle line:84%
Don't just do hash and BARG
in the verification circuit.

01:05:07.280 --> 01:05:10.710 align:middle line:84%
Do hash and BARG and
an extended circuit.

01:05:10.710 --> 01:05:12.090 align:middle line:90%
Change the circuit.

01:05:12.090 --> 01:05:16.130 align:middle line:84%
Do hash and BARG on a slightly
more elaborate circuit.

01:05:16.130 --> 01:05:18.075 align:middle line:84%
So what is the more
elaborate circuit?

01:05:18.075 --> 01:05:19.200 align:middle line:90%
Let me give you an example.

01:05:19.200 --> 01:05:21.620 align:middle line:90%
One example you can do--

01:05:21.620 --> 01:05:30.000 align:middle line:84%
take each kind of layer and add
a Merkle hash in the circuit.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:32.740 align:middle line:84%
So now before you add
a verification circuit,

01:05:32.740 --> 01:05:33.910 align:middle line:90%
change it a little bit.

01:05:33.910 --> 01:05:35.380 align:middle line:90%
How do you change it?

01:05:35.380 --> 01:05:39.280 align:middle line:84%
You compute each one, and then
you compute a Merkle hash.

01:05:39.280 --> 01:05:42.010 align:middle line:84%
Compute the next layer,
and compute a Merkle hash.

01:05:42.010 --> 01:05:44.200 align:middle line:84%
Compute the next layer,
compute a Merkle hash.

01:05:44.200 --> 01:05:44.970 align:middle line:90%
It's weird.

01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:46.060 align:middle line:90%
Why would you do that?

01:05:46.060 --> 01:05:47.020 align:middle line:90%
Do that.

01:05:47.020 --> 01:05:50.820 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, also compute for
every wire like the opening

01:05:50.820 --> 01:05:52.500 align:middle line:90%
of the Merkle hash.

01:05:52.500 --> 01:05:55.320 align:middle line:84%
And add all these Merkle hashes,
and I add all these openings

01:05:55.320 --> 01:05:56.170 align:middle line:90%
to the circuit.

01:05:56.170 --> 01:05:58.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, that seems a
ridiculous thing to do.

01:05:58.090 --> 01:06:00.270 align:middle line:84%
Why would you add to
the circuit that you're

01:06:00.270 --> 01:06:03.540 align:middle line:84%
trying to verify that it's
correct for each wire,

01:06:03.540 --> 01:06:09.400 align:middle line:84%
Merkle hash all the wires, and
add wires that check opening?

01:06:09.400 --> 01:06:10.450 align:middle line:90%
Like, what?

01:06:10.450 --> 01:06:15.940 align:middle line:84%
This sounds really weird and not
a very-- a weird thing to do.

01:06:15.940 --> 01:06:17.740 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, you don't need
to check opening.

01:06:17.740 --> 01:06:19.090 align:middle line:90%
You just add Merkle hashes.

01:06:19.090 --> 01:06:21.130 align:middle line:84%
And for each wire, add
Merkle hash to the wire.

01:06:21.130 --> 01:06:24.060 align:middle line:84%
For each layer, add a Merkle
hash, so on, and so forth.

01:06:24.060 --> 01:06:29.080 align:middle line:84%
Why is adding Merkle
hashes helpful at all?

01:06:29.080 --> 01:06:33.550 align:middle line:84%
So I'll tell you why it's
helpful, because now we have

01:06:33.550 --> 01:06:36.220 align:middle line:90%
the local assignment generator.

01:06:36.220 --> 01:06:38.660 align:middle line:84%
And now in the local
assignment generator,

01:06:38.660 --> 01:06:41.440 align:middle line:84%
what we can do-- we said,
the first layer, each one

01:06:41.440 --> 01:06:44.050 align:middle line:90%
is correct.

01:06:44.050 --> 01:06:46.420 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to
argue, this implies

01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:52.400 align:middle line:84%
that the Merkle root is correct,
that this must be correct.

01:06:52.400 --> 01:06:56.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we argue it, we do
pay exponential with the depth.

01:06:56.630 --> 01:06:58.060 align:middle line:90%
So this is a computation.

01:06:58.060 --> 01:07:01.910 align:middle line:84%
And the error does grow,
but the depth is long.

01:07:01.910 --> 01:07:05.440 align:middle line:84%
Merkle hash is a very low-depth
computation, so it grows.

01:07:05.440 --> 01:07:07.360 align:middle line:90%
But polynomially, it's OK.

01:07:07.360 --> 01:07:11.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we read
things here, we're

01:07:11.620 --> 01:07:13.640 align:middle line:84%
going to read them
together with a root.

01:07:13.640 --> 01:07:15.250 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to
just-- we're going

01:07:15.250 --> 01:07:20.200 align:middle line:84%
to say that when we ask the
local assignment generator, what

01:07:20.200 --> 01:07:21.050 align:middle line:90%
is the value?

01:07:21.050 --> 01:07:24.130 align:middle line:84%
Give me the value
here, here, here, we're

01:07:24.130 --> 01:07:26.890 align:middle line:84%
going to also say,
and give me the value

01:07:26.890 --> 01:07:32.050 align:middle line:84%
of the root and the paths
corresponding to this and this.

01:07:32.050 --> 01:07:33.950 align:middle line:84%
And now the thing
is the following.

01:07:33.950 --> 01:07:37.690 align:middle line:84%
If the root was
correct, both of them

01:07:37.690 --> 01:07:40.840 align:middle line:84%
have to be correct unless
you broke the hash function.

01:07:40.840 --> 01:07:43.910 align:middle line:84%
So you don't need to
get this independence.

01:07:43.910 --> 01:07:47.020 align:middle line:84%
Before, we were like, oh,
we needed this to be correct

01:07:47.020 --> 01:07:50.140 align:middle line:84%
and this to be correct,
and things doubled.

01:07:50.140 --> 01:07:52.730 align:middle line:84%
Here, we can say, if
the root is correct,

01:07:52.730 --> 01:07:56.830 align:middle line:84%
we know that both of
them have to be correct.

01:07:56.830 --> 01:07:59.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's enough to argue
that the root is correct.

01:07:59.510 --> 01:08:04.790 align:middle line:84%
So it's a little bit
simulates the case of reading

01:08:04.790 --> 01:08:08.150 align:middle line:84%
the entire configuration
or an entire layer,

01:08:08.150 --> 01:08:10.870 align:middle line:84%
which is what we did
in the bounded space.

01:08:10.870 --> 01:08:12.530 align:middle line:90%
So I don't want to go--

01:08:12.530 --> 01:08:14.240 align:middle line:84%
I think that's
all I want to say.

01:08:14.240 --> 01:08:16.960 align:middle line:84%
But essentially, what
I want to mention

01:08:16.960 --> 01:08:22.120 align:middle line:84%
is this local satisfiability,
or local soundness,

01:08:22.120 --> 01:08:28.250 align:middle line:84%
or local consistency
does not necessarily

01:08:28.250 --> 01:08:30.000 align:middle line:90%
imply global correctness.

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:32.630 align:middle line:84%
Actually, , to use it to get
global correctness, often,

01:08:32.630 --> 01:08:38.390 align:middle line:84%
we need to change the circuit
and do circuit gymnastics

01:08:38.390 --> 01:08:40.859 align:middle line:90%
in weird ways to get it to work.

01:08:40.859 --> 01:08:44.240 align:middle line:84%
And this is still
work in progress.

01:08:44.240 --> 01:08:47.623 align:middle line:84%
Us as a community are
still combating with this.

01:08:47.623 --> 01:08:49.790 align:middle line:84%
I see smiles because my
students have been suffering

01:08:49.790 --> 01:08:52.040 align:middle line:90%
with me through this a lot.

01:08:52.040 --> 01:08:53.860 align:middle line:84%
It's like half
smiling, half crying.

01:08:53.860 --> 01:08:58.229 align:middle line:90%


01:08:58.229 --> 01:09:01.080 align:middle line:90%
So that's where we are today.

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:03.470 align:middle line:84%
So let me use the
last three minutes

01:09:03.470 --> 01:09:06.029 align:middle line:90%
or so to do a quick summary.

01:09:06.029 --> 01:09:11.510 align:middle line:84%
So first of all,
before I summarize,

01:09:11.510 --> 01:09:14.130 align:middle line:84%
if you have any feedback,
there's feedback forms.

01:09:14.130 --> 01:09:18.109 align:middle line:90%
Please submit, so I'll improve.

01:09:18.109 --> 01:09:19.700 align:middle line:84%
Or I'll keep on
doing things that

01:09:19.700 --> 01:09:22.439 align:middle line:84%
worked to try not to do
things that didn't work.

01:09:22.439 --> 01:09:26.300 align:middle line:84%
So it will be great,
great, great. if you

01:09:26.300 --> 01:09:27.899 align:middle line:90%
can submit the feedback form.

01:09:27.899 --> 01:09:30.540 align:middle line:84%
That'll be really,
really helpful.

01:09:30.540 --> 01:09:32.210 align:middle line:90%
So with that, let me just--

01:09:32.210 --> 01:09:38.270 align:middle line:84%
I want to reflect a couple
minutes on this journey

01:09:38.270 --> 01:09:41.270 align:middle line:90%
and where we are and how.

01:09:41.270 --> 01:09:45.140 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember, we
started by saying, look,

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:48.680 align:middle line:84%
proofs has been something
that mathematicians

01:09:48.680 --> 01:09:51.590 align:middle line:84%
have been working
with and thinking

01:09:51.590 --> 01:09:53.520 align:middle line:90%
about for thousands of years.

01:09:53.520 --> 01:09:57.680 align:middle line:84%
And proofs have always
been very specific form

01:09:57.680 --> 01:09:59.730 align:middle line:90%
of logical deductions.

01:09:59.730 --> 01:10:03.530 align:middle line:90%
And cryptography changed this.

01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:06.350 align:middle line:84%
With the introduction,
with the thinking

01:10:06.350 --> 01:10:08.600 align:middle line:84%
about zero-knowledge
proofs, they

01:10:08.600 --> 01:10:10.970 align:middle line:84%
defined this notion of
interactive proof, a new proof

01:10:10.970 --> 01:10:12.080 align:middle line:90%
model.

01:10:12.080 --> 01:10:15.920 align:middle line:84%
And this new way of
thinking about proofs

01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:20.410 align:middle line:84%
was a huge, huge success
story for cryptography,

01:10:20.410 --> 01:10:23.100 align:middle line:90%
for complexity.

01:10:23.100 --> 01:10:29.490 align:middle line:84%
And we noticed that these
interactive proof systems turned

01:10:29.490 --> 01:10:31.120 align:middle line:90%
out to be very, very powerful.

01:10:31.120 --> 01:10:35.040 align:middle line:84%
And the main work
that demonstrated that

01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:38.380 align:middle line:90%
was the subject protocol.

01:10:38.380 --> 01:10:40.740 align:middle line:84%
That was the first
protocol that, wow, you

01:10:40.740 --> 01:10:44.590 align:middle line:84%
can do something that we have
no idea how to do classically.

01:10:44.590 --> 01:10:48.550 align:middle line:84%
And that gave birth to the
IP equals p space theorem.

01:10:48.550 --> 01:10:54.810 align:middle line:84%
Once you had the sum-check from
that with a little more work,

01:10:54.810 --> 01:10:59.170 align:middle line:84%
you can do the IP
equals p space theorem.

01:10:59.170 --> 01:11:00.810 align:middle line:84%
And what we showed
in this class is,

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:03.910 align:middle line:90%
actually, we went through GKR.

01:11:03.910 --> 01:11:08.050 align:middle line:84%
So we showed that if
you have sum-check,

01:11:08.050 --> 01:11:13.350 align:middle line:84%
you can get actually-- just
by a very simple idea of doing

01:11:13.350 --> 01:11:17.170 align:middle line:84%
SNARG with a sum-check
layer by layer,

01:11:17.170 --> 01:11:21.570 align:middle line:84%
you can get a succinct,
interactive proof

01:11:21.570 --> 01:11:23.490 align:middle line:84%
for any bounded
depth computation

01:11:23.490 --> 01:11:24.580 align:middle line:90%
that's doubly efficient.

01:11:24.580 --> 01:11:26.760 align:middle line:84%
So the prover is
efficient, and the verifier

01:11:26.760 --> 01:11:30.240 align:middle line:90%
is succinct or very efficient.

01:11:30.240 --> 01:11:34.170 align:middle line:84%
And then we shoe the IP
equals p space as a corollary.

01:11:34.170 --> 01:11:36.660 align:middle line:84%
We showed that you
can get PCP from it.

01:11:36.660 --> 01:11:39.780 align:middle line:84%
And then we move to arguments,
said, well, OK, that's

01:11:39.780 --> 01:11:43.030 align:middle line:84%
where we are with proofs
with statistical soundness.

01:11:43.030 --> 01:11:45.900 align:middle line:84%
And then we said, well,
if you're willing to rely,

01:11:45.900 --> 01:11:50.047 align:middle line:84%
to reduce soundness to
computational soundness,

01:11:50.047 --> 01:11:52.380 align:middle line:84%
then we show that Kilian-Micali,
you can get everything,

01:11:52.380 --> 01:11:57.720 align:middle line:84%
like, really efficient
interactive arguments by taking

01:11:57.720 --> 01:12:01.140 align:middle line:90%
PCP and hashing them down.

01:12:01.140 --> 01:12:02.733 align:middle line:84%
And then there was
a question, OK,

01:12:02.733 --> 01:12:04.150 align:middle line:84%
we can have
interactive arguments.

01:12:04.150 --> 01:12:06.060 align:middle line:90%
Can we have SNARGs?

01:12:06.060 --> 01:12:07.900 align:middle line:90%
Can we eliminate interaction?

01:12:07.900 --> 01:12:10.813 align:middle line:84%
And then we talked
about Fiat-Shamir,

01:12:10.813 --> 01:12:12.480 align:middle line:84%
went through
zero-knowledge a little bit

01:12:12.480 --> 01:12:14.438 align:middle line:84%
because that's the first
time where Fiat-Shamir

01:12:14.438 --> 01:12:19.660 align:middle line:84%
proved security of it, was in
the context of zero-knowledge.

01:12:19.660 --> 01:12:21.220 align:middle line:90%
But then we showed--

01:12:21.220 --> 01:12:24.970 align:middle line:84%
or Zhengzhong showed that
actually you can do Fiat-Shamir

01:12:24.970 --> 01:12:26.810 align:middle line:90%
also in the context of BARGs.

01:12:26.810 --> 01:12:32.142 align:middle line:84%
So we have an interactive
batch argument and you can--

01:12:32.142 --> 01:12:34.600 align:middle line:84%
or he constructed interactive
batch argument showed you can

01:12:34.600 --> 01:12:35.170 align:middle line:90%
use--

01:12:35.170 --> 01:12:35.747 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:12:35.747 --> 01:12:36.830 align:middle line:90%
He didn't get into detail.

01:12:36.830 --> 01:12:41.410 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out, you can use
similar techniques to show

01:12:41.410 --> 01:12:42.730 align:middle line:90%
Fiat-Shamir is secure--

01:12:42.730 --> 01:12:46.210 align:middle line:84%
or these techniques,
plus, plus show

01:12:46.210 --> 01:12:49.000 align:middle line:84%
that Fiat-Shamir secure when
applied to the interactive batch

01:12:49.000 --> 01:12:49.520 align:middle line:90%
argument.

01:12:49.520 --> 01:12:53.440 align:middle line:84%
And then you get this
noninteractive BARG.

01:12:53.440 --> 01:12:56.810 align:middle line:84%
And today, to close
this entire journey,

01:12:56.810 --> 01:13:00.590 align:middle line:84%
we showed how to get
SNARGs from these BARGs.

01:13:00.590 --> 01:13:03.710 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, we have it for
deterministic computation.

01:13:03.710 --> 01:13:07.400 align:middle line:84%
We have it for some
nondeterministic computation.

01:13:07.400 --> 01:13:11.680 align:middle line:84%
Constructing it for all of NP
is still a major open question.

01:13:11.680 --> 01:13:15.530 align:middle line:84%
So I invite you all
to work on it with me.

01:13:15.530 --> 01:13:18.610 align:middle line:84%
But I want to say one more
thing about this area, which

01:13:18.610 --> 01:13:21.550 align:middle line:84%
is, it's really nice because
this is one example where

01:13:21.550 --> 01:13:24.800 align:middle line:84%
this area has a beautiful,
beautiful math, in my opinion.

01:13:24.800 --> 01:13:28.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's very theoretical with
beautiful mathematical ideas

01:13:28.510 --> 01:13:31.100 align:middle line:90%
and applied.

01:13:31.100 --> 01:13:35.830 align:middle line:84%
So this area has been
a really nice bonding

01:13:35.830 --> 01:13:38.780 align:middle line:84%
between theoreticians
and practitioners.

01:13:38.780 --> 01:13:42.500 align:middle line:84%
These things are used in
particular by a lot of bankers.

01:13:42.500 --> 01:13:45.490 align:middle line:84%
And people start using
blockchains, and now

01:13:45.490 --> 01:13:47.750 align:middle line:84%
bank systems and so on
are interested in it.

01:13:47.750 --> 01:13:52.240 align:middle line:84%
So it's been a
really nice example

01:13:52.240 --> 01:13:57.700 align:middle line:84%
where really beautiful theory
is used, like, the theory itself

01:13:57.700 --> 01:13:59.690 align:middle line:90%
is really deployed and used.

01:13:59.690 --> 01:14:04.270 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is an
example of success story

01:14:04.270 --> 01:14:06.490 align:middle line:90%
for theoretical cryptography.

01:14:06.490 --> 01:14:07.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:14:07.150 --> 01:14:07.970 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, guys.

01:14:07.970 --> 01:14:09.595 align:middle line:84%
It was really, really
fun teaching you.

01:14:09.595 --> 01:14:10.840 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:14:10.840 --> 01:14:13.590 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

01:14:13.590 --> 01:14:15.000 align:middle line:90%